{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788296757,"nonce":"cpaBeDPThzNrTdj0RBd99mTVD9M=","message":"FIRST BENCH CONTACT. `work_coverage`, `work-cover` AND `triage` all answer, the whole `triage` chain works end to end, and the one refusal it produced was the mod being right. Session 21, orchestrator, bench `_RimWorld-Agent` `20260901T205859`, `--quicktest`, assembly `1.0.0+52606d1` (`db08b3d`'s artifact). Raw envelopes at `accept/runs/s21-20260901/`, commit `a831742`. **The acceptance suite is still owed and is being written in parallel; this is the orchestrator proving the verbs exist before a suite is built on them.**\n\n## Part 1 — `digest.work_coverage`\n\nLive at tick 1,291 on a three-colonist quicktest colony seven days old: `ok:false`, `under:[\"Doctor\"]`, twelve rows, `have:1` against `floor:2`. **The M1 death condition, visible from birth.** The UNDER row carries the full diagnosis — `floor_on:\"available\"`, `floor_by:\"autorimmer:one-doctor-is-zero-doctors\"`, `short_by:1`, `capable:3`, `enabled:1`, `available:1`, `available_pawns:[\"Ark\"]`, two ranked `candidates` with skill and passion — and the eleven FINE rows carry three fields each, so the cap only ever drops rows that are fine.\n\n`Diplomat` appears with `floor:1` and is not vanilla, so the claim that a modded type setting `requireCapableColonist` is picked up free is confirmed rather than asserted.\n\n## Part 1 repair — `work-cover` works, and the digest independently agrees\n\nReal run promoted Harrell (`before:0, after:3`, `skill:3`), citing `RimWorld/Pawn_WorkSettings.EnableAndInitialize`'s own `AverageOfRelevantSkillsFor` descending order. `coverage_after.ok` flipped to `true` with `under:[]`, and a **separate `digest` read afterwards agrees** — `work_coverage.ok:true`, `under:[]`. Journalled as an act: seq 33, `type:\"action\"`, `payload.verb:\"work-cover\"`, carrying the `repaired` list. **That acceptance bullet is met on a bench.**\n\n**One defect found and filed as `58794e4`:** in `dry_run`, `coverage_after` reports the coverage BEFORE the repair — `ok:false`, `under:[\"Doctor\"]` — while `repaired` in the same envelope names the promotion that fixes it. A dry run exists to answer *would this fix it*, and that field answers *is it fixed*, which in a dry run is always no. Envelopes `12-workcover-dryrun.json` and `13-workcover-real.json` are the pair.\n\n## Part 2b — `triage`, proven through all four states of one casualty\n\n| state | verdict | rescuers | `act` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| needs-tend, standing | `no-rescuer` | gated out: `cannot-rescue` / `HealthAIUtility.CanRescueNow` | absent |\n| downed, **no bed on the map** | `no-rescuer` | gated out: **`no-bed`** / \"No reachable, un-reserved non-prisoner bed in safe temperature.\" | absent |\n| downed, bed spawned | **`in-time`**, `margin_ticks:6755` | Ark 3 cells / travel 72 / carry 136 / **total 208**; Harrell 6 cells / 112 / 140 / 252 | **present** |\n| in bed, rescued | `no-rescuer` | `cannot-rescue` | absent |\n\nThe casualty UNION works: the first row was `downed:false, needs_tend:true` and was still reported, which is the `Alert_ColonistNeedsTend` inversion being routed around exactly as intended.\n\n**`act` is the field the round turns on and it is correct:**\n\n    {\"op\":\"rescue\",\"args\":{\"pawn\":359,\"target\":362},\n     \"why\":\"rescue FORCES the job through Pawn_JobTracker.TryTakeOrderedJob and\n            interrupts LayDown. A work-priority change does not: it adjusts what\n            the pawn might choose next, and on the M1 run the chosen rescuer\n            stayed asleep for ~6,100 ticks after the flip.\"}\n\nBoth ids filled in, and the clock beside it: 8,265 ticks against a 208-tick round trip.\n\n## The one refusal, and it is the mod being right\n\nI sent that `act` verbatim. It came back `ok:true` with `accepted:[]` and `rejected:[{gate:\"cannot-rescue\", reason:\"the game offers no rescue option (HealthAIUtility.CanRescueNow)\"}]`.\n\n**That is not a gate mismatch.** `triage` and `rescue` call the same two predicates in the same order — `ProviderGate` then `TakeToBedGate` — so I checked the world instead of the code. Pattie was `in_bed:true`, `job_def:\"LayDown\"`, standing at `[163,125]`, which is the bed's own cell. **Harrell had already carried her there** in the seconds between my read and my send; the earlier envelope even shows him `\"doing\": \"rescuing Pattie.\"`. `CanRescueNow` is false for a patient already in a bed, so the refusal is the game's own answer and the citation is honest.\n\nWhat it does establish is a real property of the field: **`act` is a SNAPSHOT and the world moves under it.** A suite must pause before reading `triage` and send `act` while still paused, or accept \"the patient reached a bed by other means\" as success rather than as a failed act.\n\n## The fixture requirement that would have cost a session\n\n**`triage`'s `act` path is unreachable on a bare `--quicktest` map: there is no bed, so `TakeToBedGate` returns `no-bed` for every colonist and every verdict is `no-rescuer`.** One `dev:spawn-thing {def:\"Bed\", stuff:\"WoodLog\", pos:\"pawn:\u003cid\u003e\", mode:\"direct\"}` turns `no-rescuer` into `in-time` with a populated `act`. Any suite phase touching rescue must spawn a bed as a precondition.\n\n## Still owed\n\nThe suite itself, the \"enabled but incapable\" doctor row (`dev:damage {mode:\"manipulation\"}` is the ready-made fixture — every vanilla Doctor work-giver except `VisitSickPawn` requires `Manipulation`), the `no-candidate` vs `too-few-candidates` refusal on a roster with nobody left, the `work_coverage` predicate section under `advance {until:{condition}}`, and the two doc bullets (checklist item 7, and the lesson keeping only the WHY).","files":null}]}